# SRG-APP-000231-WSR-000144  -  CAT II
# Information at rest must be encrypted using a DOD-accepted algorithm to
# protect the confidentiality and integrity of the information.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000231-WSR-000144
group:           V-206407
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      tested
cci:             CCI-001199
control_rev5:    SC-28

contribution:    For the data Splunk Enterprise itself encrypts, the application can
                 constrain the algorithms used by operating in FIPS mode, and where index
                 buckets are stored on a remote object store it selects the server-side
                 encryption scheme for data at rest. Splunk does not encrypt index data
                 held on local disk; that is the storage layer's work.

governs:         indexes.conf - The indexes data is stored in, how large they may grow and how long data
                 is kept.
governs:         splunk-launch.conf - Environment settings read before Splunk starts, including whether it
                 starts in FIPS mode.

check:           splunk-launch.conf [] SPLUNK_FIPS in 1|true|t|yes (matched case-insensitively)
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
corrected:       splunk-launch.conf [] SPLUNK_FIPS equals 1 -> splunk-launch.conf [] SPLUNK_FIPS in 1|true|t|yes (matched case-insensitively)
vendor:          indexes.conf [volume:<volume_name>] remote.s3.encryption https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/manage-splunk-enterprise-indexers/10.4/deploy-smartstore/smartstore-on-s3-security-strategies
vendor:          splunk-launch.conf [] SPLUNK_FIPS https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/splunk-launch.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Encryption of index data held on local disk, which Splunk Enterprise does
                 not perform - file system or block device encryption provided by the
                 operating system or storage layer
external:        The accepted-algorithm determination for the object store's server-side
                 encryption and the keys behind it - cloud key management service and
                 organizational policy
external:        Encryption of configuration files, lookups and saved search artifacts on
                 local disk - operating system or storage layer
external:        Identification of which stored data requires protection - organizational
                 data classification
external:        Encryption of index data held on local storage, which no indexes.conf
                 value expresses; remote.s3.encryption applies to a remote volume, and this
                 instance defines none, so an unconditional test on it reports a finding
                 against a volume that does not exist.

citation:        splunk-launch.conf.spec:110-116 (SPLUNK_FIPS = [0|1], FIPS Publication
                 140-2 algorithms and restrictions); indexes.conf.spec:2411-2420
                 ([volume:<volume_name>] remote.s3.encryption = sse-s3 | sse-kms | sse-c |
                 cse | none, "The encryption scheme to use for data buckets that are
                 currently being stored (data at rest)"); indexes.conf.spec:2786-2791
                 (remote.gs.encryption = gcp-sse-c | gcp-sse-kms | gcp-sse-gcp - every
                 permitted value is an encryption scheme, so no condition is needed);
                 authorize.conf.spec:544 ([capability::edit_encryption_key_provider]).
                 Excluded as not implemented in this release: distsearch.conf.spec:557-559
                 (remote.s3.encryption, "Currently not supported. This setting is related
                 to a feature that is still under development") and
                 indexes.conf.spec:2440-2453 (remote.s3.encryption.cse.algorithm and
                 remote.s3.encryption.cse.key_type, both marked "Currently not supported").
finding:         Data at rest is not protected to the extent the product allows: FIPS mode
                 is not enabled, or a remote index volume is configured with its encryption
                 scheme set to none.
